      "math_academy_id": "q-86605",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4d1ea5c4-2700-5c03-a22e-de5439f47c36",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing a Quotient of Complex Numbers in Polar Form",
      "problem": "Express $z=\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{2}{1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{(1+\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\text{i})}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=1-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=1$ is positive and $y=-1$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))\n$$",
